update public.courses set description='An introduction to customer needs, markets, consumer behaviour and the marketing decisions used to create and deliver value.',reading_plan='{"overview":"This plan introduces marketing as a customer-focused process rather than simply selling or advertising. Study each concept using familiar Nigerian products and services, then practise applying the marketing mix to a defined target market. Compare your revision coverage with your lecturer’s course outline.","modules":[{"title":"Week 1-2: Meaning and development of marketing","topics":["Needs, wants and demand","Customer value and exchange","Production, product, selling and marketing orientations","Marketing and organisational functions"],"tasks":["Read introductory marketing notes and define core concepts.","Compare a sales-focused business with a customer-focused business."]},{"title":"Week 3-4: Marketing environment and research","topics":["Microenvironment and macroenvironment","Economic, social, technological and legal influences","Primary and secondary data","Marketing research process and ethics"],"tasks":["Identify environmental influences on a business in Port Harcourt.","Draft five neutral questions for a customer survey and explain consent."]},{"title":"Week 5-6: Consumer behaviour and market selection","topics":["Consumer buying decision process","Cultural, social, personal and psychological influences","Market segmentation bases","Targeting and positioning"],"tasks":["Map the stages in a student’s laptop purchase.","Develop a target segment and positioning statement for a campus service."]},{"title":"Week 7-8: Product and pricing decisions","topics":["Goods, services and product benefits","Branding, packaging and labelling","Product life cycle","Pricing objectives and basic approaches"],"tasks":["Compare the branding and packaging of two local products.","Calculate a cost-plus price and discuss whether customers would accept it."]},{"title":"Week 9-10: Distribution and promotion","topics":["Marketing channels and intermediaries","Retailing, wholesaling and distribution intensity","Advertising, sales promotion and personal selling","Public relations and integrated marketing communications"],"tasks":["Draw distribution channels for packaged food and a digital service.","Prepare a promotional plan with a consistent message for one target market."]},{"title":"Week 11-12: Services, digital marketing and responsibility","topics":["Service characteristics and the extended marketing mix","Customer satisfaction and relationship marketing","Digital channels and basic performance measures","Consumer protection, privacy and ethical marketing"],"tasks":["Evaluate people, process and physical evidence in a campus clinic.","Review a sample advertisement for misleading claims and privacy concerns."]}],"key_terms":[{"term":"Marketing","definition":"The process of creating, communicating, delivering and exchanging offerings that provide value."},{"term":"Need","definition":"A basic condition of felt deprivation, such as hunger or the need for safety."},{"term":"Demand","definition":"A want for a specific offering supported by willingness and ability to buy."},{"term":"Customer value","definition":"The customer’s assessment of benefits received relative to monetary and non-monetary costs."},{"term":"Market segmentation","definition":"Dividing a market into groups with similar needs, characteristics or behaviours."},{"term":"Target market","definition":"The customer group or groups an organisation chooses to serve."},{"term":"Positioning","definition":"Developing a distinct place for an offering in the minds of target customers relative to competitors."},{"term":"Marketing mix","definition":"The controllable product, price, place and promotion decisions used to serve a target market."},{"term":"Brand","definition":"A name, symbol, design or other feature that identifies and differentiates an offering."},{"term":"Customer satisfaction","definition":"The customer’s assessment of how an offering’s perceived performance compares with expectations."}],"exam_tips":["Distinguish needs, wants and demand with separate examples.","Apply the four Ps to a specific product and target segment.","Do not confuse segmentation, targeting and positioning.","Use examples to distinguish advertising, sales promotion and public relations.","For case questions, link recommendations to customer needs, costs, ethics and measurable objectives."]}'::jsonb where slug='rsu-ehr-115';
) v(p,q,a,b,c,k) where c.slug='rsu-ehr-115';
update public.courses set description='An introduction to sociological explanations of work, industrial organisation and employment relationships in society.',reading_plan='{"overview":"This course examines work as a social activity shaped by institutions, power, technology and inequality. Compare the main theories, learn their key concepts, and apply them to formal and informal workplaces in Nigeria.","modules":[{"title":"Week 1-2: Sociology and the Meaning of Work","topics":["Sociological perspective","Work, employment and occupation","Formal and informal economic activity","Work, identity and social institutions"],"tasks":["Define work, employment and occupation with separate examples.","Compare paid employment with unpaid household work."]},{"title":"Week 3-4: Industrialisation and Classical Perspectives","topics":["Industrialisation and factory production","Marx: class and alienation","Durkheim: division of labour and solidarity","Weber: rationalisation and bureaucracy"],"tasks":["Create a comparison table of Marx, Durkheim and Weber.","Explain two social changes associated with factory production."]},{"title":"Week 5-6: Organisation and Workplace Social Relations","topics":["Formal and informal organisation","Authority, hierarchy and rules","Scientific management and human relations","Organisational culture and work-group norms"],"tasks":["Draw a formal organisational chart and identify possible informal networks.","Contrast scientific management with the human relations approach."]},{"title":"Week 7-8: Inequality, Labour Markets and Occupations","topics":["Social stratification and class","Gender and occupational segregation","Education, skills and social mobility","Formal employment, informal work and unemployment in Nigeria"],"tasks":["Analyse barriers to entry into a skilled occupation.","Discuss gender segregation using examples without treating stereotypes as natural facts."]},{"title":"Week 9-10: Conflict, Control and Employment Relations","topics":["Managerial control and worker responses","Trade unions and collective action","Strikes, bargaining and workplace cooperation","Unitarist and pluralist perspectives"],"tasks":["Compare unitarist and pluralist explanations of workplace conflict.","Analyse a hypothetical pay dispute and possible negotiated solutions."]},{"title":"Week 11-12: Technology and Changing Work","topics":["Automation and changing skills","Platform work and employment insecurity","Globalisation and labour migration","Work-life balance and occupational wellbeing"],"tasks":["Assess two possible effects of automation on workers.","Write a revision essay linking platform work, control and insecurity."]}],"key_terms":[{"term":"Sociology of work","definition":"The study of work, workplace relationships and their connections with wider society."},{"term":"Division of labour","definition":"The allocation of different tasks or roles among people or groups."},{"term":"Alienation","definition":"In Marx’s analysis, workers’ separation from control over their labour, its products and human potential."},{"term":"Bureaucracy","definition":"An organisational form characterised by formal rules, specialised offices and a hierarchy of authority."},{"term":"Informal organisation","definition":"Unofficial relationships, networks and norms that develop among organisational members."},{"term":"Social stratification","definition":"The structured ranking of social groups with unequal access to resources and opportunities."},{"term":"Occupational segregation","definition":"The concentration of social groups in different occupations or levels of employment."},{"term":"Informal employment","definition":"Employment not adequately covered, in law or practice, by formal employment arrangements and protections."},{"term":"Labour process","definition":"The organisation and control of work through which labour produces goods or services."},{"term":"Precarious work","definition":"Work marked by insecurity, uncertain continuity or limited employment protection."}],"exam_tips":["Compare theorists by their explanations of order, power and conflict rather than listing names.","Use Nigerian examples to illustrate concepts, not as substitutes for definitions.","Distinguish an informal workplace group from informal employment in the economy.","Avoid assuming all technology necessarily removes jobs or always improves job quality.","Structure essays around a clear argument, relevant theory, examples and a short conclusion."]}'::jsonb where slug='rsu-ehr-123';
) v(p,q,a,b,c,k) where c.slug='rsu-ehr-123';
update public.courses set description='An introduction to the coordination of sourcing, inventory, transport, warehousing and information flows across supply chains.',reading_plan='{"overview":"This plan explains how logistics activities support the wider coordination of suppliers, organisations and customers. Follow a product from sourcing to final delivery, and practise simple inventory, cost and performance calculations. Use Nigerian transport and distribution examples while checking topic emphasis against your lecturer’s outline.","modules":[{"title":"Week 1-2: Logistics and supply chain foundations","topics":["Logistics and supply chain management","Upstream and downstream relationships","Material, information and financial flows","Customer service and total logistics cost"],"tasks":["Draw a supply chain for locally packaged rice.","Explain how logistics supports customer service and organisational goals."]},{"title":"Week 3-4: Procurement and supplier relationships","topics":["Purchasing and procurement","Procurement cycle and specifications","Supplier evaluation and selection","Ethical sourcing and supplier relationships"],"tasks":["Draft a simple specification and purchase requisition for office supplies.","Compare suppliers using quality, delivery reliability and total cost."]},{"title":"Week 5-6: Inventory and demand planning","topics":["Raw materials, work-in-progress and finished goods","Inventory costs and stockout risk","Lead time, safety stock and reorder point","Demand forecasting and the bullwhip effect"],"tasks":["Calculate reorder points from daily demand, lead time and safety stock.","Use a simple moving average to forecast demand and discuss its limits."]},{"title":"Week 7-8: Warehousing and materials handling","topics":["Receiving, storage, picking and dispatch","Warehouse layout and safe handling","FIFO and FEFO stock rotation","Inventory records and cycle counting"],"tasks":["Sketch a warehouse flow that separates receiving and dispatch safely.","Choose suitable stock rotation rules for food, medicines and stationery."]},{"title":"Week 9-10: Transportation and distribution","topics":["Road, rail, water, air and pipeline transport","Mode selection and transport cost-service trade-offs","Distribution networks and third-party logistics","Delivery routing and last-mile challenges"],"tasks":["Compare transport modes for urgent medicines and bulk non-urgent cargo.","Explain how congestion and road conditions affect deliveries in Port Harcourt."]},{"title":"Week 11-12: Integration, performance and sustainability","topics":["Information sharing and logistics technology","Order accuracy and on-time delivery measures","Disruptions, resilience and risk management","Reverse logistics and environmental responsibility"],"tasks":["Calculate on-time delivery and inventory turnover from a short data set.","Develop a response plan for supplier delays and a process for handling returns."]}],"key_terms":[{"term":"Logistics","definition":"Planning, implementing and controlling the movement and storage of goods and related information to meet customer needs."},{"term":"Supply chain management","definition":"Coordination of sourcing, conversion and logistics activities within and across organisations."},{"term":"Procurement","definition":"The process of identifying needs and obtaining goods or services from external suppliers."},{"term":"Lead time","definition":"The elapsed time between initiating an activity, such as placing an order, and its completion or receipt."},{"term":"Inventory","definition":"Stocks of materials or goods held for use, processing or sale."},{"term":"Safety stock","definition":"Extra inventory held to reduce the risk of stockouts caused by uncertain demand or supply."},{"term":"Reorder point","definition":"The inventory position at which a replenishment order should be placed."},{"term":"Warehousing","definition":"Receiving, storing, protecting, picking and dispatching goods at a storage facility."},{"term":"Third-party logistics","definition":"The use of an external provider to perform logistics activities for an organisation."},{"term":"Reverse logistics","definition":"Managing flows of returned products or materials from users towards recovery, reuse, recycling or disposal."}],"exam_tips":["Explain how logistics differs from, but forms part of, supply chain management.","Label upstream and downstream flows clearly in diagrams.","Show formulas and keep demand and lead-time units consistent in calculations.","Discuss total cost and service trade-offs rather than selecting the cheapest transport automatically.","Use practical cases to connect procurement, inventory, warehousing, transport and customer service."]}'::jsonb where slug='rsu-lsm-101';
) v(p,q,a,b,c,k) where c.slug='rsu-lsm-101';